  "text": "**Solution 1 (t−1) is wrong.** Any saturated G must contain t−1 disjoint P₄'s, since adding one edge raises the P₄-packing by at most one. So ρ(G) ≥ ρ((t−1)P₄) ≈ 1.618 for t = 2, which rules out t−1 = 1.\n\n**Solution 3 (formula at n = 8, t = 2 gives 5) is wrong.** Take G = K₃ ∪ K₅ on 8 vertices:\n- G is 2P₄-free, because each P₄ needs 4 vertices from the K₅ and two disjoint ones need 8 vertices there.\n- Every non-edge joins K₃ to K₅, so by symmetry it suffices to check one. Adding a₁b₁ gives the disjoint paths a₂–a₃–a₁–b₁ and b₂–b₃–b₄–b₅, so G is saturated.\n- ρ(K₃ ∪ K₅) = max(2, 4) = 4 < 5.\n\n**Solution 2 (n − 3t + 2) is wrong for large n.** At t = 2 it claims 96 for n = 100. The graph K₃ ∨ K̄₉₇ is 2P₄-free, since every P₄ must use at least 2 of the 3 universal vertices. Each non-edge v_a v_b is saturated by v_c–u₁–v_a–v_b together with v_d–u₂–v_e–u₃. Its spectral radius is 1 + √292 ≈ 18.08, well below 96. So the claimed minimum is not the minimum, even though its value at n = 8 happens to be 4.\n\nNone of the three final answers is correct.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
